{-
   What a structural class refuses, and how it says so.

   # resolver: ownership

   `Structural.yana` is the accepting half and runs in both stages; this one names a resolver because
   the two word the refusal differently, and the difference is the point. The shipping stage reports
   a missing instance - "no instance of TrivialCopy for (Guard)" - which is what selection says when
   the table has nothing. This stage answers *before* the table and therefore knows the difference
   between "nothing declares it" and "the compiler has already decided otherwise", so it says the
   second: a classification the compiler makes from the type is not one a program can fix by writing
   an instance.

   Writing the instance the message declines to suggest is refused where instances are declared, by
   `isStructuralClass`'s third reader - and it cannot be spelled here anyway, since a class with no
   members has no body to indent under the `:` the parser wants.
-}

data Guard {id: Int}

instance Drop(Guard):
    fn drop(->value: Guard) -> {} = {}

data Holder {guard: Guard}

fn (TrivialCopy(a)) copyable(x: a) -> Int = 1

-- A type with a teardown of its own.
fn direct() -> Int = copyable(Guard {id: 1})

-- And a record that holds one, which is the fold rather than a second rule.
fn held() -> Int = copyable(Holder {guard: Guard {id: 2}})

fn main() -> Int = direct() + held()
